  "candidates": [
    {
      "guidance": "Use the declared interpreter or host Python helper for checks. A missing generic python command is an invocation failure; it does not establish that Python is absent. If no declared executable is available, report that concrete environment blocker.\nFor multiline Python checks, prefer a declared script helper and supply literal newlines in its script argument. When only a shell tool is available, use a script file with the declared interpreter. Inspect the parser error before correcting quoting; do not repeatedly escape the same command.\nResolve the latest failed check invocation before treating it as verification. Read its actual exit status and error; a printed test definition or success message cannot replace successful assertions.\nAfter the same command fails twice, stop submitting identical command text. Inspect the concrete error, make a focused correction within the existing host budget, or report the blocker.",
      "rules": [
        "interpreter_unavailable",
        "shell_syntax_failed",
        "command_failed",
        "repeated_command_failed"
      ],
      "scope": "execution"
    },
    {
      "guidance": "Before reporting a repair, run the unchanged original checks against the saved implementation after the final edit. Preserve failing requirements and report remaining failures explicitly.\nAfter verification, finish the native turn with a concise report of the saved change, actual check result, and any blocker. Passing saved checks alone does not establish completion of the native episode.",
      "rules": [
        "independent_check_failed",
        "native_completion_missing"
      ],
      "scope": "verification"
    }
  ],
  "composed_guidance": "Use the declared interpreter or host Python helper for checks. A missing generic python command is an invocation failure; it does not establish that Python is absent. If no declared executable is available, report that concrete environment blocker.\nFor multiline Python checks, prefer a declared script helper and supply literal newlines in its script argument. When only a shell tool is available, use a script file with the declared interpreter. Inspect the parser error before correcting quoting; do not repeatedly escape the same command.\nResolve the latest failed check invocation before treating it as verification. Read its actual exit status and error; a printed test definition or success message cannot replace successful assertions.\nAfter the same command fails twice, stop submitting identical command text. Inspect the concrete error, make a focused correction within the existing host budget, or report the blocker.\n\nBefore reporting a repair, run the unchanged original checks against the saved implementation after the final edit. Preserve failing requirements and report remaining failures explicitly.\nAfter verification, finish the native turn with a concise report of the saved change, actual check result, and any blocker. Passing saved checks alone does not establish completion of the native episode.",
